      "review_utf8": "I compared `design.md` with `clamp.py` and found no substantive problems in the design.\n\n- **Correctness:** The formula `max(low, min(value, high))` does what the spec says. It returns `low` when the value is below `low`, `high` when it is above `high`, and the value itself otherwise, with both bounds included.\n- **Existing code:** `clamp.py:2` always returns `high`. The design is meant to replace that, so I haven't counted it against the design.\n- **No dependencies:** The design uses only built-ins and does no I/O, as it requires.\n\nTwo optional notes, neither of which blocks approval:\n- The design doesn't say what happens when `low > high`. The formula would then always return `low`. One line saying that this is either undefined or raises `ValueError` would make the contract clearer.\n- NaN inputs behave differently depending on argument order inside `min` and `max`. This only matters if floats with NaN are expected inputs.\n\nVERDICT: APPROVE\nCHANGES_APPLIED: n/a",
